return await window.__createIslands([{"id":"ess-27","kind":"prose","x":3200,"y":2200,"w":460,"html":"<div style=\"font-family:'nimbus sans l',sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#000\"><p>A virtual model of an exhibition space can begin as substitute, but it need not remain one. When physical installation becomes impossible, when travel fails, when a pandemic closes rooms, when the intended site is available only through plans, measurements, photographs, and memory, the model becomes a site in its own right. It is not simply a lesser version of an absent gallery. It is an architectural proposition constructed from distance, a technical fiction that preserves some relations while inventing others. In such a model, devices may be arranged, signals visualised, cables extended, lights added, landscapes displaced, and relations staged that would otherwise remain unrealised. The absent exhibition becomes both loss and method. This is significant for artistic research because it refuses the hierarchy in which physical installation is real and simulation is secondary. Both are constructed environments. Both require conventions of access. Both can stage invisible forces only through apparatus. The virtual installation makes this explicit. It shows that the site of the work is not identical with physical location, but with the set of relations through which perception, model, infrastructure, and imagination are brought into operative contact.</p></div>"},{"id":"ess-28","kind":"prose","x":3800,"y":2200,"w":460,"html":"<div style=\"font-family:'nimbus sans l',sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#000\"><p>In the virtual installation, the found object returns as data. A transmitter, receiver, server rack, cable, lamp, architectural fragment, or generic technical device may arrive as a stock model: authored elsewhere, circulated through platforms, stripped of context, and made available for recombination. The readymade has become downloadable, but it has not become neutral. Stock models carry assumptions about function, realism, scale, interface, technological culture, and the generic appearance of infrastructure. They are already miniature ideologies of use. To place them in an artistic work is not merely to populate a scene. It is to work with the sedimented visual language of technical environments. The stock model is both convenient and symptomatic. It offers a ready-made image of apparatus at precisely the moment when apparatus itself is under interrogation. Treated carefully, it can become a computational cousin of the found object, not because it is physically displaced from everyday life into art, but because it is conceptually displaced from functional depiction into speculative infrastructure. It asks what kind of world has already been modelled for us, and how those models condition our sense of what technical reality looks like.</p></div>"},{"id":"ess-29","kind":"prose","x":4400,"y":2200,"w":460,"html":"<div style=\"font-family:'nimbus sans l',sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#000\"><p>The sine wave is one of modernity's quiet emblems. It is mathematical, electrical, acoustic, visual, pedagogical, decorative, and infrastructural. It describes oscillation, but it also disciplines it, rendering fluctuation as elegance and periodicity as form. In the works considered here, the sine wave functions as a bridge between registers: sound, colour, signal, model, and transmission. A frequency may be sounded, coloured, transmitted, calculated, displayed, or imagined. Yet these registers are not equivalent. Translation between them is productive precisely because it is imperfect. When a waveform becomes sound, something is gained and something is betrayed. When frequency becomes colour, analogy risks becoming false equivalence. When a signal becomes animation, mathematical relation becomes retinal event. The point is not to claim that sound, colour, and radio frequency are the same, but to explore how technical culture routinely converts between domains in order to produce experience. The sine wave becomes a figure for that conversion: a shared contour across incompatible senses and scales.</p></div>"}]);